llama.cpp (as fetched by Ollama v0.35.1, llama.cpp b11232, applied after
135): the gfx803 prompt flash-attention kernels for head sizes 96, 128 and
256 (patches 130 and 131) start their blocks with the query heads of one
K/V head side by side, instead of every query tile of one head in a row.
The results are the same bits.
gfx803-specific: these kernels run only on GCN4.

WHY
---
A block of these kernels covers 64-96 query rows of one head and writes
its output rows to dst, where consecutive query rows of a head lie n_head
x D floats apart: 8 KiB for 16 heads at D 128, 16 KiB for the partial
results of a split K/V range. On an RX 590 an address stride that large
maps every row to the same DRAM channel. Blocks start in launch order with
the query tile fastest, so the ~36 blocks in flight belonged to one head
and all of their stores queued on one channel. With 512 keys and a
2048-query batch (head size 128, 4 K/V heads at GQA 4) the kernel took
2.96 ms, of which the output stores cost 0.67 ms (16.8 MB, about 25 GB/s);
split over two key ranges it took 6.8 ms plus 1.4 ms to combine, which is
why patch 130 avoids the split unless the GPU would sit mostly idle.

WHAT
----
Each block derives its query tile and head from its linear launch index
with the heads of one K/V head varying fastest. Those heads' rows lie D
floats apart, which spreads the stores over the channels, and they read
the same K and V tiles, so the blocks in flight still share them in L2.
The arithmetic of every block is unchanged.

Result on an RX 590, test-backend-ops perf (f16 K/V, mask, us per call
including the combine of split results):

    head 128, 4 K/V heads at GQA 4, 512 keys, 2048 queries
      one block per output tile                 3056 ->  2473
      K/V range split over two blocks           8552 ->  3958
    head 128, 8 K/V heads at GQA 4, 4096 keys, 4096 queries
                                               71797 -> 69446
    head 256, 2 K/V heads at GQA 16 (Qwen3.6 35B-A3B), 10000 keys, 512 queries
      run alone                                 47241 -> 46859
      inside a sweep of head-256 cases          86255 -> 47131

In a real prompt, Qwen3.6 35B-A3B in 512-token batches 8192 tokens deep
(llama-bench, rocprof), the head-256 kernel takes 9.76 ms per call instead
of 10.03. At that depth the compute of a block outweighs its stores, so
prompt speed is unchanged within noise on Qwen3 8B and Qwen3.5 9B
(llama-bench -p 512 -d 8192). The split K/V range is now cheap, but this
patch leaves patch 130's rule for when to split as it is: splitting to fill
the last wave as well gains 4-5% at 16k-128k keys and loses up to 74% at
512.

Logits are byte-identical: llama-perplexity over 1,024 tokens in 512-token
batches on Qwen3.6 35B-A3B, Qwen3 8B and Qwen3.5 9B writes the same file
with and without the patch.

Verified on an RX 590: test-backend-ops test -b ROCm0 -o FLASH_ATTN_EXT,
with the head-96/128/256 prompt cases added (6,555 in all), gives the same
result with and without the patch: all pass but one, head 128 with 113 keys
and two sequences, where a fully masked row comes out NaN instead of 0 when
the KV range is split. That failure is in the split-result combine, not in
this kernel; patch 138 fixes it, and with both all 6,555 pass.

To reproduce, build llama.cpp b11232 with GGML_HIP=ON and
AMDGPU_TARGETS=gfx803, then with and without the patch:

    test-backend-ops perf -b ROCm0 -o FLASH_ATTN_EXT -p "hsk=128,hsv=128,nh=8"

